@page "/"

<PageTitle>DbContext scope demo</PageTitle>

<h1>DbContext scope demo</h1>

<p class="lead">
    This sample shows what happens when several Blazor Server components share one scoped EF Core
    <code>DbContext</code>, then compares three safe ways to avoid overlapping work on that instance.
</p>

<div class="row g-4">
    <div class="col-lg-6">
        <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h4 card-title">Shared scoped context</h2>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Several child components call the same circuit-scoped service during initialization. Because that
                    service also holds one scoped <code>DbContext</code>, EF Core can throw its
                    <code>A second operation was started...</code> exception.
                </p>
                <a class="btn btn-danger" href="scoped-dbcontext-repro">Open example</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-lg-6">
        <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h4 card-title">Factory-created context</h2>
                <p class="card-text">
                    The service lifetime stays scoped, but every lookup creates its own <code>DbContext</code> through
                    <code>IDbContextFactory&lt;TContext&gt;</code>, so sibling components stop sharing one instance.
                </p>
                <a class="btn btn-primary" href="dbcontext-factory-comparison">Open example</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-lg-6">
        <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h4 card-title">Serialized access</h2>
                <p class="card-text">
                    The service still shares one scoped <code>DbContext</code>, but a gate allows only one EF Core
                    operation at a time. Safe, but queued.
                </p>
                <a class="btn btn-info" href="serialized-scoped-dbcontext">Open example</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-lg-6">
        <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h4 card-title">Component-owned scope</h2>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Each child component owns its own DI scope, so the scoped service and scoped <code>DbContext</code>
                    stop being shared across siblings.
                </p>
                <a class="btn btn-outline-primary" href="component-owned-scope">Open example</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
